te]} {:db/id "q-318660/field-7" :answer-field/id #uuid "40bd2010-6217-5d2d-b657-2927261b465f" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-318660/field-7/aa2c7adcefb2e0c9301467d38adcb0254c3081264e590d07785a2af8f6000b56" :answer-field/choices ["q-318660/field-7/a1b7f00bae21be24be7faafec77c4d8076ac1818ff0604f47c3cee5ea8f03cd7" "q-318660/field-7/aa2c7adcefb2e0c9301467d38adcb0254c3081264e590d07785a2af8f6000b56" "q-318660/field-7/08f23e98e73473f5daefd17a7254a1ae752b6989d94facef957b64ebb7d3a534"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-318660"] :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3})=3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(3{y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -(3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1/y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{y}^{-3})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(-1/y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}\\,{e}^{-1/y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-318660/field-1/ma-v1/c2073ac4c9ee340ec0b0a6e59f1eab15bc5831a92b20ca8d02aef3aeb6c4fe0f" "q-318660/field-2/ma-v1/72f6a5a2e4b1ba885ab0a71ecd66b45621643ba48fcdc332dd623c6a6e7c0a64" "q-318660/field-3" "q-318660/field-4" "q-318660/field-5" "q-318660/field-6" "q-318660/field-7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337400/field-1/f52f1181020cd909d273610d01eaf5c2431b6b1c503197f19b4ad00c5edc1dcf" :answer/id #uuid "ae9ac284-1606-542c-97b2-7ad864ab5f6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337400/field-1/a4e70f1e09c087ba355e2403b61fd39309a3080369b40c8e9a74a3aedf3b8e63" :answer/id #uuid "fe7750c6-388c-5974-94ee-45e2bca12cf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac