r/id #uuid "95447100-a3db-5303-aa62-26cedbb3ca41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(2xy\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337400/field-7/14e78ebe2b5f0eeb943a46a53acfa9f9c4ba11eb8eef0a1b19c72b6dafe61cbb" :answer/id #uuid "63c18cfd-cdec-5c3d-a7cc-e75b91611008" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}(2xy\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337400/field-7" :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb592d3-016f-5deb-b68c-c1bdd126b99e" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337400/field-7/14e78ebe2b5f0eeb943a46a53acfa9f9c4ba11eb8eef0a1b19c72b6dafe61cbb" :answer-field/choices ["q-337400/field-7/cf2a25ee830a3532be3eb7b35ec5ce39344bb60a5d38d2e74367c94464484b19" "q-337400/field-7/a92faa200af82b299440c5184983dc5294855faf03d9b6f93256d0c863559cda" "q-337400/field-7/f2d147641c2432dbd39f39edeea488d2ecbbce7b9067735740ada5ec05d3bc31" "q-337400/field-7/14e78ebe2b5f0eeb943a46a53acfa9f9c4ba11eb8eef0a1b19c72b6dafe61cbb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337400" :question/id #uuid "8455dd35-3ef6-5817-8bd5-bd6711371ecf" :question/math-academy-id "q-337400" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n2xy\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we conclude that an integrating factor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the differential equation by the integrating factor gives\n\n$$\n{e}^{{x}^{2}}(2xy\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337400/field-1" "q-337400/field-2" "q-337400/field-3" "q-337400/field-4" "q-337400/field-5" "q-337400/field-6" "q-337400/field-7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cbedeed-6ac6-5ed8-bd25-9a85eda9577b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337400"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337476/field-1/f52f1181020cd909d273610d01eaf5c2431b6b1c503197f19b4ad00c5edc1dcf" :answer/id #uuid "3f335fa9-8436-5fa8-bf15-f22fd8326631" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337476/field-1/1dac212d79310fa0d55cc5566e30bdb01ac612bb2