db/id "q-337476/field-8/6e1b4196d508f0f95aa1ad310202ce381942c520c3bba56a6558738abc86b3fa" :answer/id #uuid "a141ea21-787d-5197-bc95-1dc0153e1c5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}((2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337476/field-8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c431362-6a62-5441-927e-177427b5de5e" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337476/field-8/7e782b101289dd99153bcfc222f7b0a330692eab3132282dfa26e5bee64f7af7" :answer-field/choices ["q-337476/field-8/51fca627703d4ab200911c1a2e2df1078ccf0b9646cdde4948607baa8d14be17" "q-337476/field-8/8cf2b4e1e5895b55b83a4bcdc2576077410d63cdd48fddb34434d4a2f7242fcb" "q-337476/field-8/7e782b101289dd99153bcfc222f7b0a330692eab3132282dfa26e5bee64f7af7" "q-337476/field-8/6e1b4196d508f0f95aa1ad310202ce381942c520c3bba56a6558738abc86b3fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337476" :question/id #uuid "c9f9f6ec-3614-51f5-8a8c-4b576b94a5aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-337476" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y-2\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-3y,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-3y,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-3y)=-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{1}(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot ((2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3x}\\cdot ((2-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337476/field-1" "q-337476/field-2" "q-337476/field-3" "q-337476/field-4" "q-337476/field-5" "q-337476/field-6" "q-337476/field-7" "q-337476/field-8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cbedeed-6ac6-5ed8-bd25-9a85eda9577b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337476"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
